## Inputs, actions and events

| Role | Application responsibility | During model execution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `input` | Implement the read interface below, or supply a snapshot for this cycle | Sampled anew each cycle; read-only to the model, no implicit hold |
| `param` | Supply during construction/initialization; omissions use DSL defaults | Fixed per instance; generated getters need no callback implementation |
| `control` (`def`, often called var) | Optionally preset at initialization, then observe | Model-owned writable state; holds when not assigned |
| `output` | Read after a successful cycle and pass to an actuator if needed | Shares persistent storage with control; holds when not assigned |

`control` and `output` share persistent `vars` storage. Both keep their value when a cycle does not assign them. Read results through `vars`; writing internal storage from application code is unsupported and can invalidate verified model properties. Outputs do not invoke external setters or actuator callbacks.

Pass instance configuration through `parameters` when constructing the machine. Values are validated and copied; changing the original mapping has no effect. Cold construction uses each omitted parameter's DSL default. Hot construction requires complete parameters and persistent values. Parameters have no cycle argument or setter; `parameters` and each generated `get_param_*()` are read-only.

Implement the generated `read_*()` methods to acquire external inputs. The runtime reads each input once per cycle, including inputs not used by the chosen path, and freezes that sample across validation and execution. `get_input_*()` is valid only during a cycle and never calls the device again. Construction and ended cycles do not read inputs. A subsequent cycle acquires a new sample; no implicit hold applies to inputs.

`cycle(inputs=...)` supplies explicit values instead of invoking the corresponding read hooks. Missing entries use their hooks. Unknown names and invalid values are errors. A failed acquisition or execution preserves persistent values and `last_inputs`; a successful cycle, including a Delta cycle, publishes a new read-only `last_inputs` snapshot. Before the first successful cycle it is `None`. Action contexts expose independent read-only `inputs` and `parameters` snapshots.

{% if model.inputs or model.parameters %}
For this model, the integration methods are:

| Role | DSL name | Method |
| --- | --- | --- |
{% for item in model.parameters.values() %}| param | `{{ item.name }}` | `get_param_{{ item.name }}()` |
{% endfor %}{% for item in model.inputs.values() %}| input | `{{ item.name }}` | implement `read_{{ item.name }}()`; model reads `get_input_{{ item.name }}()` |
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
The `read_*()` input interface is separate from the `_abstract_hook_*()` lifecycle action hooks. If an input has neither an explicit value nor an implemented reader, its default reader raises `NotImplementedError`. An `int` input requires a Python `int`; a `float` input accepts finite `int`/`float` values. Neither accepts `bool`. Invalid input values raise `ValueError`; exceptions from your device reader propagate. `cycle()` returns `None`: consume/send outputs only after normal return. Failure does not undo device reads or other external side effects that have already occurred.

### Callback responsibilities

| Interface | When it runs | Application contract |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `read_<input>()` | Once per active cycle unless that input has an explicit value | Return one typed reading; device errors propagate |
| `_abstract_hook_* (ctx)` | At the declared lifecycle action, during execution only | Observe context or perform application work; default hooks do nothing |
| `cycle(events=...)` | At the host scheduling boundary | Submit event paths; this is separate from numeric input acquisition |

Extend the `Device` class above for action integration. When actions are declared, use the exact generated methods listed below; defining an unrelated method does not register an action. `ref` calls invoke their resolved abstract target. Hooks may inspect `ctx.vars`, `ctx.inputs` and `ctx.parameters`; use the context for values at that action, and read `machine.vars` after the cycle for committed results.

